   "subject": "Shiny apps do not run locally on our R Stduio Server",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">I posted this question a couple of weeks ago on Stack Overflow, but it did not get many views and no one had any answers.   I wonder if there might be more interest over here.</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">I have never been able to make Shiny apps run locally  — in any way, shape or form — on the R Studio server on CentOS at our College.  Here’s an example.  From a fresh R session, enter:</p>\r\n<pre style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;font-size:1em;line-height:1.2em;margin:1.2em 0px\"><code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);background-color:rgb(248,248,248);border-radius:3px;display:inline;white-space:pre;overflow:auto;border-radius:3px;border:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding:0.5em 0.7em;display:block!important;display:block;padding:0.5em;color:rgb(51,51,51);background:none repeat scroll 0% 0% rgb(248,248,255)\"> shiny::runApp(system.file(&quot;examples/06_tabsets&quot;, package=&quot;shiny&quot;))\r\n</code></pre><p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">In the console you get:</p>\r\n<pre style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;font-size:1em;line-height:1.2em;margin:1.2em 0px\"><code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);background-color:rgb(248,248,248);border-radius:3px;display:inline;white-space:pre;overflow:auto;border-radius:3px;border:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding:0.5em 0.7em;display:block!important;display:block;padding:0.5em;color:rgb(51,51,51);background:none repeat scroll 0% 0% rgb(248,248,255)\"> Loading required package: shiny\r\nListening on <a href=\"http://127.0.0.1:5925\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://127.0.0.1:5925&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792394152032000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3UflKa0ibZ_8g24RJdY-5P\">http://127.0.0.1:5925</a>\r\n</code></pre><p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">and then a separate browser window opens up.  After  a moment the UI appears in the window, but before anything appears from the “server” part of shiny, the window vanishes.</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">A similar problem crops up in related context, e.g., when trying to run a Shiny R Markdown document (either in Pane or in separate window).</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">Here is the CentOS version info:</p>\r\n<pre style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;font-size:1em;line-height:1.2em;margin:1.2em 0px\"><code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);background-color:rgb(248,248,248);border-radius:3px;display:inline;white-space:pre;overflow:auto;border-radius:3px;border:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding:0.5em 0.7em;display:block!important;display:block;padding:0.5em;color:rgb(51,51,51);background:none repeat scroll 0% 0% rgb(248,248,255)\">    &gt; system(&quot;cat /etc/*release*&quot;)\r\n    CentOS release 6.6 (Final)\r\n    LSB_VERSION=base-4.0-amd64:base-4.0-noarch:core-4.0-amd64:core- 4.0-noarch:graphics-4.0-amd64:graphics-4.0-noarch:printing-4.0-amd64:printing-4.0-noarch\r\n    cat: /etc/lsb-release.d: Is a directory\r\n    CentOS release 6.6 (Final)\r\n    CentOS release 6.6 (Final)\r\ncpe:/o:centos:linux:6:GA\r\n</code></pre><p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">We are running R Studio 0.98.1062.  The R session info is:</p>\r\n<pre style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;font-size:1em;line-height:1.2em;margin:1.2em 0px\"><code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);background-color:rgb(248,248,248);border-radius:3px;display:inline;white-space:pre;overflow:auto;border-radius:3px;border:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding:0.5em 0.7em;display:block!important;display:block;padding:0.5em;color:rgb(51,51,51);background:none repeat scroll 0% 0% rgb(248,248,255)\">    &gt; sessionInfo()\r\n    R version 3.1.2 (2014-10-31)\r\n    Platform: x86_64-redhat-linux-gnu (64-bit)\r\n\r\n    locale:\r\n    [1] C\r\n\r\n    attached base packages:\r\n    [1] stats     graphics  grDevices utils     datasets  methods   base     \r\n\r\n    loaded via a namespace (and not attached):\r\n    [1] tools_3.1.2\r\n</code></pre><p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">I have my own R Studio Server as an Amazon EC2 micro-instance, running on Ubuntu 14.04.  Shiny apps always run locally, there.</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">It seems unlikely that the problem is due to the CentOS operating system.  Perhaps there is a particular way that the firewall must be configured?</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">If anyone has experienced a similar problem, your insights would be much appreciated.</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">Homer</p>\r\n<div title=\"MDH:SSBwb3N0ZWQgdGhpcyBxdWVzdGlvbiBhIGNvdXBsZSBmbyB3ZWVrcyBhZ28gb24gU3RhY2sgT3Zl\r\ncmZsb3csIGJ1dCBpdCBkaWQgbm90IGdldCBtYW55IHZpZXdzIGFuZCBubyBvbmUgaGFkIGFueSBh\r\nbnN3ZXJzLiDCoCBJIHdvbmRlciBpZiB0aGVyZSBtaWdodCBiZSBtb3JlIGludGVyZXN0IG92ZXIg\r\naGVyZS48YnI+PGJyPkkgaGF2ZSBuZXZlciBiZWVuIGFibGUgdG8gbWFrZSBTaGlueSBhcHBzIHJ1\r\nbiBsb2NhbGx5wqAgLS0gaW4gYW55IHdheSwgc2hhcGUgb3IgZm9ybSAtLSBvbiB0aGUgUiBTdHVk\r\naW8gc2VydmVyIG9uIENlbnRPUyBhdCBvdXIgQ29sbGVnZS7CoCBIZXJlJ3MgYW4gZXhhbXBsZS7C\r\noCBGcm9tIGEgZnJlc2ggUiBzZXNzaW9uLCBlbnRlcjo8YnI+PGJyPmBgYDxicj7CoHNoaW55Ojpy\r\ndW5BcHAoc3lzdGVtLmZpbGUoImV4YW1wbGVzLzA2X3RhYnNldHMiLCBwYWNrYWdlPSJzaGlueSIp\r\nKTxicj5gYGA8YnI+PGJyPkluIHRoZSBjb25zb2xlIHlvdSBnZXQ6P